er hands.\n\nSo, I'm folding.  Gotta save those chips for a better spot.\n\n**Evaluation:**\n\nThe opponent's large river overbet (nearly 4x the pot) is highly polarizing, representing either a very strong hand (like a straight or set) or a bluff. I hold a pair of 3s (bottom pair), which is a weak hand. Given the poor pot odds and the opponent's line, calling requires a high probability of opponent bluffing, which is unlikely.\n\n**Reason:**\n\nThe pot odds are unfavorable. My hand strength (bottom pair) is insufficient to call a large overbet.\n\n**Answer:** -1\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The opponent has made a massive overbet on the river, betting 795 into a pot of roughly 200 (Action sequence: Round 0 pot 200, checks until river). This is nearly a 4x pot bet. My hand is a pair of 3s with an Ace kicker (bottom pair on the board K-9-8-6-3). This betting line (Check-Check-Check-Huge Bet) is typically polarized, representing either the nuts (a straight with a 7, or a set) or a bluff. Given the board texture, a 7 is a very possible holding for the opponent. To call profitably, I would need to win approximately 44% of the time. It is unlikely that an opponent is bluffing with this specific sizing often enough to make a call with bottom pair profitable. Folding is the risk-averse and theoretically sound play here.\n\nReason: The bet size offers very poor pot odds, and my hand (bottom pair) is too weak to act as a bluff catcher against such a large bet without specific reads on the opponent. Folding preserves chips for better opportunities.\n\nAnswer: -1'}
