ons are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '**River Decision Analysis: Check or Bet?**\n\nOkay, here\'s the deal. I\'m on the river. Board\'s paired with Jacks, and I\'ve got a Queen kicker. Small pot, everyone\'s been checking all the way. My gut says check, but let\'s break it down like a pro.\n\nFirstly, my hand\'s a bit of a tricky one.  I\'ve got a pair of jacks, but they\'re on the board! My Queen kicker, while good, doesn\'t really matter against A, K, a better pair (22-AA), or two pairs on board. It\'s a "bluff catcher" at best, and it doesn\'t beat a lot of hands.\n\nThe main concern is, what does my opponent have?  Four checks in a row screams weakness, but it could be a trap. If they\'ve got Trips, or even a weak two pair, they\'ll likely have played it differently. They could just have a low card, maybe a draw that missed, or even a pocket pair under the jacks.\n\nBetting is a bluff, essentially. If I bet, maybe I get A-high or K-high to fold. Small pairs will likely call. If I check, I lose to any A/K high, and a pair but beat T, 9 or 7.\n\nSince I\'m barely winning overall, I should play safe. Checking means I win most of the time without risk, and avoid giving chips away to a bad hand. I may lose to a king or an ace, which is a significant part of their potential range.\n\nPlus, I’m a bit worried about checking back the turn when the 3rd club hit; maybe the opponent put me on a flush draw? And the board is dangerous: Any pair (22, 55, 88) or an A or K means I have the worse hand. They\'ll likely call (and beat me), which is what I need to avoid with this marginal hand.\n\nGiven the small pot, checking protects my chips, allows a cheap showdown with a fair chance of winning, and avoids potential value-owning. Let’s take the free showdown and try to come out ahead. I’m checking.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: The current situation is a limped or checked-down pot with a board of 8c 2c Jd Jc 5d. My hand is 3c Qd. My final hand strength is a Pair of Jacks (from the board) with a Queen kicker (J-J-Q-8-5). This hand loses to any Ace or King (which would play as a higher kicker), any pocket pair (which would make two pair or better), and any card matching the board (8, 5, 2) which would make two pair. I only beat lower high-card hands (Ten-high or lower). However, the opponent has checked on every street (Pre-flop, Flop, Turn, River), which strongly suggests they have a very weak hand or a missed draw. While betting might fold out an Ace or King high, it is risky because the opponent might call with a low pair (like a 2 or 5) given the passive action, and the pot is very small (likely 200 chips). Checking allows me to realize my showdown value for free, potentially winning against Ten-high or missed straight draws, while avoiding losing extra chips to a slightly better hand like King-high or a weak Two Pair.\n\nReason: My hand has marginal showdown value (Board Pair + Q kicker). The pot is small, and the opponent's passive line suggests weakness. Betting is unlikely to fold out better hands like small pairs, and checking avoids the risk of being called by better or raised. I will check to see the showdown cheaply.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
